---
import Corners from './Corners.astro';

const stack = [
  {
    group: 'AI & vision',
    items: ['PyTorch', 'timm', 'scikit-learn', 'SciPy', 'OpenCV', 'Segment Anything', 'ONNX Runtime'],
  },
  {
    group: 'Models',
    items: ['EfficientNetV2', 'ViT', 'CAFormer', 'ConvNeXt', 'YuNet', 'SFace'],
  },
  {
    group: 'Software',
    items: ['Python', 'NumPy', 'pandas', 'FastAPI', 'Pydantic', 'Git', 'Google Colab', 'Kaggle GPUs'],
  },
  {
    group: 'Hardware & embedded',
    items: ['Raspberry Pi 4B', 'STM32F411', 'RS-485', 'Modbus RTU', 'I2C', 'KiCad'],
  },
];
---

<section id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <h2 class="label" id="about-title"><span class="no">01</span> about</h2>
  <p>
    Aviation has always been the pull. The long-term goal is to become an aircraft maintenance engineer, and the road
    there runs through IoT and embedded systems: a modern aircraft is full of them, and keeping one healthy is more and
    more a sensors-and-data problem. So for now it's small boards, tight power budgets, and models that have to earn
    their place in memory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside the lab my free time goes to two very different pets: a growing collection of cacti that ask for almost
    nothing, and predatory fish that ask for a lot more. When I can get away I travel, and somehow I always end up near
    water: a waterfall, a river, the sea. And I'm learning French, slowly.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sub mono">Outside the lab</h3>
  <Corners />

  <h3 class="sub mono">How I work</h3>
  <p>
    If a model scores suspiciously well, I go looking for the shortcut before I celebrate. That habit is how I
    caught AquaVision-X reading the background instead of the fish. Before each run I write down what I
    expect, I log every seed, and I keep the runs that went badly, so anyone can check my numbers.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also try to prove a part actually does something before I trust it: once, randomizing a whole 85.8M-parameter
    branch changed nothing, which told me it had been dead all along. On the hardware side I'd rather solve a problem at
    the system level than buy a faster chip, and safety belongs in firmware, not in Python.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sub mono">Stack</h3>
  <p class="note muted">
    Hardware items are the SmartGate design stack: in the design and bench-lab phase, not yet field-proven.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        {
          stack.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.group}</th>
              <td>{row.items.join(' · ')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="meta muted">
    <span class="mono">Interests</span> computer vision · IoT · signal processing · 5G networks<br />
    <span class="mono">Languages</span> Vietnamese · English (IELTS 6.5) · French (getting there)
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .sub {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: 28px 0 10px;
  }
  .note {
    font-size: 14px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .meta {
    font-size: 14px;
    margin-top: 18px;
    line-height: 1.9;
  }
  .meta .mono {
    font-size: 11.5px;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-right: 8px;
  }
  th[scope='row'] {
    white-space: nowrap;
    width: 1%;
  }
</style>
